  "mcp.catalog.title": "Connect a service",
  "mcp.entry.github.summary": "Read repositories, issues and pull requests — the code and the conversation around it.",
  "mcp.entry.github.containment": "Read-only is on, which the server enforces above every other setting. Nothing is stored: the sign-in token lives in the server's memory and never reaches disk or Chimera.",
  "mcp.entry.githubBinary.summary": "The same GitHub server, run directly instead of through Docker.",
  "mcp.entry.githubBinary.containment": "Read-only is on. The sign-in happens inside the server, so the token never reaches Chimera; with no browser available it falls back to a device code.",
  "mcp.entry.firebase.summary": "Query Firestore, Auth, Storage and the rest of a Firebase project.",
  "mcp.entry.firebase.containment": "It acts as whoever ran firebase login on this machine, so that account's own permissions are the boundary. There is no read-only switch — the per-tool marks are hints to the client, not something the server enforces.",
  "mcp.entry.supabase.summary": "Read a Supabase project: schema, data, edge functions and its documentation.",
  "mcp.entry.supabase.containment": "Read-only and narrowed to database and docs, both enforced by Supabase rather than by a hint the client may ignore. Sign-in happens in the browser, so there is no key to store. It reaches the server through the third-party mcp-remote bridge.",
  "mcp.entry.db.summary": "Inspect and query a {n} database: table names, schemas, and SQL.",
  "mcp.entry.db.containment": "This server has NO read-only mode and commits every statement immediately — a DROP runs. What limits it is the database user in the connection URL, so point it at one with only the grants you are willing to lose.",
